1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,880
Look at the biggest question parts in the

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:04,720
last five years of HSC Chemistry and Biology

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,840
papers, the ones worth seven marks or more.

4
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:10,320
Of the seventeen that used a glossary key

5
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:14,000
word, ten asked you to analyse, evaluate or

6
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:15,080
justify.

7
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:17,360
And in seven of the last twelve marking

8
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:21,200
feedback reports, NESA flagged the same weakness: a

9
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:24,320
judgement without enough reasons from the evidence.

10
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:26,560
So let's learn to make a call, and

11
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:27,440
back it up.

12
00:00:28,667 --> 00:00:31,306
Here's what NESA's glossary says, in short.

13
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:35,306
Analyse means break something into parts, show how

14
00:00:35,347 --> 00:00:37,706
they connect, and work out what follows.

15
00:00:38,267 --> 00:00:42,826
Assess means judge the value, quality, outcomes, results

16
00:00:42,907 --> 00:00:44,186
or size of something.

17
00:00:44,747 --> 00:00:48,506
Evaluate means judge against criteria, or work out

18
00:00:48,587 --> 00:00:49,146
the value.

19
00:00:49,707 --> 00:00:52,746
Justify means give support for an argument or

20
00:00:52,827 --> 00:00:53,626
a conclusion.

21
00:00:54,187 --> 00:00:56,106
Three of them end in a judgement, a

22
00:00:56,187 --> 00:00:58,186
call you make or support.

23
00:00:58,307 --> 00:01:00,186
All four need reasons.

24
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,880
Here's an HSC-style Chemistry question.

25
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,320
About fifty millilitres of one mole per litre

26
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:10,440
hydrochloric acid has spilled on a lab bench.

27
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,720
You could neutralise it with calcium carbonate powder,

28
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:15,960
or with sodium hydroxide solution.

29
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:17,720
A table compares them.

30
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:21,000
The question asks you to evaluate using calcium

31
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:23,720
carbonate rather than sodium hydroxide.

32
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:25,080
Four marks.

33
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:27,640
If you take Biology, watch the shape of

34
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:30,120
the answer: your question is coming.

35
00:01:31,334 --> 00:01:32,293
Run the routine.

36
00:01:32,374 --> 00:01:35,493
The verb is evaluate, so you'll end with

37
00:01:35,534 --> 00:01:37,893
a judgement, tested against criteria.

38
00:01:38,454 --> 00:01:41,453
Four marks suggests about four linked points.

39
00:01:41,734 --> 00:01:44,493
The phrase rather than sodium hydroxide is a

40
00:01:44,534 --> 00:01:48,133
constraint: every point weighs one against the other.

41
00:01:48,374 --> 00:01:50,933
And the table rows are your evidence: use

42
00:01:51,014 --> 00:01:52,533
them, don't just copy them.

43
00:01:53,734 --> 00:01:57,253
An assess or evaluate answer needs three parts.

44
00:01:57,494 --> 00:02:00,533
A verdict: your judgement, how good or how

45
00:02:00,694 --> 00:02:01,413
suitable.

46
00:02:01,654 --> 00:02:04,173
Criteria: what you're judging by.

47
00:02:04,374 --> 00:02:07,413
Here that's safety, what an excess does, and

48
00:02:07,494 --> 00:02:08,533
knowing when to stop.

49
00:02:08,934 --> 00:02:11,733
And evidence: the details from the question that

50
00:02:11,814 --> 00:02:12,693
show each one.

51
00:02:13,174 --> 00:02:15,733
Here's the memory aid: no verdict without a

52
00:02:15,814 --> 00:02:18,773
reason, and no reason without evidence.

53
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,400
Start with the verdict, so the marker knows

54
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:25,200
where you're going: calcium carbonate is the more

55
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:26,480
suitable choice.

56
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:29,200
Then criterion one, safety.

57
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:33,120
Calcium carbonate is low hazard, whereas sodium hydroxide

58
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:35,480
is corrosive to skin and eyes.

59
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:38,480
Criterion two, what happens if you add too

60
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:39,200
much.

61
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:42,960
Calcium carbonate is insoluble, so extra just sits

62
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:45,200
there as a solid, and the mixture stays

63
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:46,320
near pH eight.

64
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,920
Extra sodium hydroxide would leave a strongly basic

65
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
spill, above pH twelve.

66
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:56,160
Criterion three, knowing when to stop.

67
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:59,520
Calcium carbonate fizzes as it reacts, and the

68
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:02,200
fizzing stops when the acid is used up.

69
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:05,120
Sodium hydroxide gives no sign.

70
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:08,480
Now weigh a limitation, because a fair evaluation

71
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:12,400
looks both ways: calcium carbonate is slower, and

72
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:14,360
it gives off carbon dioxide.

73
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:15,920
Then close the loop.

74
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:19,120
For a small spill, safety and control matter

75
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:22,000
more than speed, so calcium carbonate is the

76
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:23,000
better choice.

77
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:23,920
That's four.

78
00:03:25,134 --> 00:03:26,813
Now here's a weaker answer.

79
00:03:27,454 --> 00:03:30,973
"Calcium carbonate is better than sodium hydroxide because

80
00:03:31,014 --> 00:03:33,053
it is safer and it is a weak

81
00:03:33,134 --> 00:03:36,493
base." There's a verdict, and even a because.

82
00:03:36,974 --> 00:03:40,253
But "safer" never says why, and nothing comes

83
00:03:40,334 --> 00:03:41,053
from the table.

84
00:03:41,694 --> 00:03:43,853
Weak base is true, but it never says

85
00:03:43,934 --> 00:03:45,213
why that matters here.

86
00:03:45,854 --> 00:03:47,613
In our marking guide it earns one of

87
00:03:47,774 --> 00:03:49,693
four, for a relevant judgement.

88
00:03:50,174 --> 00:03:52,573
It announces the answer without earning it.

89
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:56,920
NESA's marking guidelines show both halves matter.

90
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:00,760
In one recent six-mark evaluate question, a judgement

91
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:03,320
first appears at four marks, and the top

92
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:06,120
bands asked for an informed judgement, based on

93
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:07,400
analysis of the data.

94
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,240
In a four-mark evaluate question, describing the effects

95
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:13,560
for both cases reached three.

96
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:16,200
Only evaluating them reached four.

97
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:19,720
In both, answers without a judgement stopped short

98
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,880
of the top, and no band paid for

99
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:23,240
a verdict on its own.

100
00:04:24,467 --> 00:04:27,426
So how is assess different from evaluate?

101
00:04:27,827 --> 00:04:30,946
They overlap: both need a supported judgement.

102
00:04:31,187 --> 00:04:34,306
Evaluate leans on criteria: name what you judge

103
00:04:34,387 --> 00:04:37,306
by, and weigh strengths against limitations.

104
00:04:37,507 --> 00:04:40,946
Assess leans on how much, or how good:

105
00:04:41,027 --> 00:04:43,546
you'd lead with how suitable, and how far.

106
00:04:43,747 --> 00:04:46,146
Either way, write all three parts.

107
00:04:46,387 --> 00:04:49,586
Justify asks for the support: whether the conclusion

108
00:04:49,667 --> 00:04:51,746
is given or you choose it, back it

109
00:04:51,827 --> 00:04:53,506
with reasons from the case.

110
00:04:53,587 --> 00:04:57,266
In one recent justify question, identifying two changes

111
00:04:57,387 --> 00:05:00,306
earned the same as fully justifying one.

112
00:05:00,387 --> 00:05:02,226
The reason was worth as much as a

113
00:05:02,387 --> 00:05:03,266
second change.

114
00:05:04,467 --> 00:05:05,106
Your turn.

115
00:05:05,187 --> 00:05:07,946
Do the one in your subject, or both.

116
00:05:08,227 --> 00:05:09,186
Chemistry first.

117
00:05:09,827 --> 00:05:11,666
A buffer with a pH of about four

118
00:05:11,827 --> 00:05:13,186
point eight is needed.

119
00:05:13,747 --> 00:05:16,306
Ethanoic acid has a pKa of four point

120
00:05:16,467 --> 00:05:17,746
seven four.

121
00:05:17,787 --> 00:05:20,146
The ammonium ion has a pKa of nine

122
00:05:20,307 --> 00:05:21,426
point two five.

123
00:05:21,987 --> 00:05:25,106
Justify which acid, with its conjugate base, should

124
00:05:25,187 --> 00:05:26,626
be used to make the buffer.

125
00:05:27,107 --> 00:05:27,826
Two marks.

126
00:05:28,307 --> 00:05:29,506
Pause here to answer.

127
00:05:39,934 --> 00:05:43,133
Verdict: ethanoic acid with ethanoate.

128
00:05:43,614 --> 00:05:46,493
Reason: a buffer works best when its pH

129
00:05:46,574 --> 00:05:49,453
is close to the acid's pKa, because then

130
00:05:49,494 --> 00:05:51,773
the acid and its conjugate base are in

131
00:05:51,934 --> 00:05:54,893
similar amounts, ready for added acid or base.

132
00:05:55,374 --> 00:05:58,733
Evidence: four point seven four is almost exactly

133
00:05:58,814 --> 00:06:01,533
four point eight, but nine point two five

134
00:06:01,614 --> 00:06:03,533
is over four units away.

135
00:06:03,854 --> 00:06:07,053
The habit to drop: "its pKa is closer,"

136
00:06:07,134 --> 00:06:09,373
with no reason why closer matters.

137
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:12,040
Now Biology.

138
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:14,920
Part of an mRNA sequence has the codon

139
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,880
G A A, for glutamic acid.

140
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:21,320
Mutation one changes it to G A G,

141
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:23,000
still glutamic acid.

142
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:26,600
Mutation two changes it to U A A,

143
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,320
a stop codon.

144
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:31,880
A student claims that a substitution mutation always

145
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:33,080
changes the protein.

146
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:36,040
Using the information, assess this claim.

147
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:37,240
Three marks.

148
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:39,000
Pause here to answer.

149
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:53,000
Verdict first: the claim is not supported, because

150
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:54,520
of the word always.

151
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:58,120
Mutation one gives G A G, still glutamic

152
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:00,520
acid, so the protein stays the same.

153
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:04,280
Mutation two makes a stop codon, so translation

154
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:06,760
ends early and the polypeptide is shorter.

155
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:10,600
Some substitutions change the protein, and some don't.

156
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:14,520
The habit to drop: labelling them silent and

157
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:16,200
nonsense, then stopping.

158
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:18,360
That never judges the claim.

159
00:07:19,567 --> 00:07:21,806
So, when you see one of these verbs.

160
00:07:22,367 --> 00:07:25,486
For assess or evaluate, make a judgement and

161
00:07:25,567 --> 00:07:28,566
support it with relevant criteria and evidence.

162
00:07:28,847 --> 00:07:32,206
For justify, support the argument or conclusion with

163
00:07:32,287 --> 00:07:33,806
reasons from the case.

164
00:07:34,047 --> 00:07:37,646
For analyse, show the parts, their relationships and

165
00:07:37,727 --> 00:07:38,966
what follows.

166
00:07:39,407 --> 00:07:42,766
Next: calculations, and how to set out working

167
00:07:42,807 --> 00:07:44,046
a marker can follow.

168
00:07:45,267 --> 00:07:46,706
Here are the key notes.

169
00:07:46,947 --> 00:07:48,866
Pause the video if you need more time

170
00:07:48,947 --> 00:07:49,746
to copy them.
